[Start "kings-gambit-fischer 4...g5"]

5. Nc3 { Develops and watches d5 and e4. The engine prefers this to the sharper h4 because the knight on f3 is not yet pushed around. } 5... Bg7 ( 5... g4 6. Bxf4 { Gives up the knight for two pawns, because the g-pawn attacks it. The bishop on f4 and the open f-file give White a strong attack. } 6... gxf3 7. Qxf3 ) 6. g3 { Attacks f4 from the other side so that White regains the pawn and opens the g-file for the rook. } 6... Nc6 7. d5 { Kicks the knight and gains space. It leaves the knight on c6 only one good square, e5. } 7... Ne5 8. gxf4 { Takes the f4 pawn back, with the g-file opening for White's rook. } 8... gxf4 9. Bxf4 { Wins the second pawn back. White's pieces are all active and Black's king has not castled. } 9... Bg4 10. Bg2 { Develops the bishop on the long diagonal and prepares castling. Plan: trade Black's active knight on e5, castle, and use the f-file and open g-file against the king. The engine likes Black a little, as it does in most King's Gambit lines. } 10... Nxf3+ 11. Bxf3 Qf6 12. O-O { Castles into safety and brings the rook to the open f-file. } *
